## Ordering of lists in the native subset (type.coll.11, type.num.11,
## expr.cmp.1): `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `.cmp()` on a list compare element by
## element, the first difference deciding, a prefix before the longer list.
## The same word kinds order the elements of `sorted`, `min` and `max`: a
## list of lists adds 8 to its elements' kind (`List[List[float]]` is 10),
## and `contains` and `==` use the same kinds. Elements are `int`, `char`,
## sized integers, `str` and `float`; the runtime is `rt.list_cmp` and the
## kinds of `list_word_lt` and `list_word_eq` in `rt/native/list.ail`.
use ast

## The kind that orders and compares the words of a list of `e`, or -1:
## 0 integers (and `char`), 1 strings, 2 floats, and 8 more for a list of
## those. A `bool` list stores bytes, so it has no kind here.
fn ord_kind(e Ty) int
    match e
        Int | Char | Sized(_) => 0
        Str => 1
        Float | F32 => 2
        List(inner) =>
            k := ord_kind(inner)
            if k < 0 or k >= 8 then -1 else k + 8
        _ => -1

## `a < b` and its kin on two lists of one type.
fn Lower.list_order(mut self, o ast.BinOp, a Val, b Val) Val?
    e := a.ty.elem()?
    k := ord_kind(e)
    name := op_name(o)
    if k < 0 or not is_comparison(o) or name == "eq" or name == "ne"
        return none
    c := call("rt.list_cmp", [a.id, b.id, konst(k, Ty.Int)], Ty.Int)
    Val(id: op(name, [c, konst(0, Ty.Int)], Ty.Bool), ty: Ty.Bool)

## `a.cmp(b)` on two lists of one type, as an `Ordering`.
fn Lower.list_cmp_method(mut self, a Val, b Val, pos ast.Pos) Val?
    e := a.ty.elem()?
    k := ord_kind(e)
    if k < 0 or not same_ty(a.ty, b.ty) or not enums.contains("Ordering")
        return unsupported(pos, "`.cmp()` on `{a.ty.name()}`")
    c := call("rt.list_cmp", [a.id, b.id, konst(k, Ty.Int)], Ty.Int)
    ordering(op("add", [c, konst(1, Ty.Int)], Ty.Int))
